0255} {:answer/id #uuid "04909b9f-93e2-5df7-b90b-6edd4f5b129b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{1}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390256} {:answer/id #uuid "27ef3511-79f4-5a6f-ad05-8b54ca9eb346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{1}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edbf70a5-1c06-5125-97ea-5807e444ad04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{1}(3\\cdot 1-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390255} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a82d898-0fed-5e57-803f-4563d8b4f784" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2734feef-c61f-5f93-a156-2b91d9513029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{k+1}(3k-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390259} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a0e041-3d34-5503-b9c4-4d6b5d5ecefc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{k+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390260} {:answer/id #uuid "60db97ee-e30d-5168-b835-4b2cebc9afcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{k+1}(3k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390261} {:answer/id #uuid "e2616432-2799-5c6a-97c4-2ca50da8c798" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{k+1}(3k-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60db97ee-e30d-5168-b835-4b2cebc9afcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{3}^{k+1}(3k+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390261} :answer-field/id #uuid "73abbcc4-d3be-5486-9e48-71327d778226" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "796d95ca-bb93-5537-834d-cf21102e920d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183886" :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-9{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=36,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using strong induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2),\\,∀n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2)$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2)$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=6{a}_{n}-9{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={3}^{1}(3\\cdot 1-2) \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={3}^{2}(3\\cdot 2-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2)$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={3}^{k}(3k-2) \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={3}^{k-1}(3(k-1)-2)={3}^{k-1}(3k-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={3}^{k+1}(3(k+1)-2)={3}^{k+1}(3k+1)\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={3}^{n}(3n-2)$ for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac5337bb-d568-5b4b-97df-c5b4595aa852" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f336ac5c-c2d4-5d91-8207-c57d6c540df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{1}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390265} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ad60b2-cb73-5fbb-97fa-a7862c610e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390266} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3e322d-f0db-59bf-9b2d-3f6aa93e9239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f336ac5c-c2d4-5d91-8207-c57d6c540df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{9}^{1}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390265} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3eee55b-e532-565e-b0a4-d0e6f68ab5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adbbf950-76a0-5ed0-878d-16768e635067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer