 Cases**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}} \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={9}^{n}-7$ is true for all {{field-3}} for {{field-4}}. In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}={{field-5}} \\\\ {a}_{k-1}={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={9}^{n}-7$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={9}^{n}-7\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=10{a}_{n}-9{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={9}^{1}-7 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={9}^{2}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={9}^{n}-7$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={9}^{k}-7 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={9}^{k-1}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={9}^{k+1}-7.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={9}^{n}-7$ for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6ea26-5bab-5804-9bf9-da3f91adaa7d" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253c8946-2acc-52d7-b729-b773d382ec0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390296} {:answer/id #uuid "38b93688-bc55-54ad-b880-d8c1d92e6618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{k-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390297} {:answer/id #uuid "3d515ae9-aeee-5be9-96ad-4a7be5c7266c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{2}^{k}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390298} {:answer/id #uuid "acb632e3-a008-54b4-815d-78f295589823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{k-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acb632e3-a008-54b4-815d-78f295589823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{k-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390299} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72bc307-f080-5a0c-830d-e92bf52abd28" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a729762-badd-5260-a20d-8a8b942ad269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390301} {:answer/id #uuid "57d6e55b-a7a5-58e1-8d5f-f7b6967ff530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #