/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06489dcb-a2a6-5ca8-bdbf-cfce4c6f2ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390312} :answer-field/id #uuid "543c55a0-0e3a-5e0e-b5bd-a4160e2f0ad4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8213bc6f-ea3a-5837-9244-a8b6d2858155" :question/math-academy-id "q-183919" :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{a}_{1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{1-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=3\\cdot {2}^{k-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{(k+1)-1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{n-1}$ for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6ea26-5bab-5804-9bf9-da3f91adaa7d" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Proof Template"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4820dab4-6c2f-5744-b4c0-260df9179ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390315} {:answer/id #uuid "63533fcf-cbb1-57b0-920e-43aa1fae08dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390316} {:answer/id #uuid "0618ee2f-c709-5ed3-90b1-9cd125f0eeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0618ee2f-c709-5ed3-90b1-9cd125f0eeff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390317} :answer-field/id #uuid "b24b8084-436b-52f3-88fd-9eb87b39c606" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390314} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab94fbe0-e610-56ab-92dc-d360f774fd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390319} {:answer/id #uuid "9bdc3170-5800-5005-80a5-41f4085c10cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390320} {:answer/id #uuid "d100f7ec-2178-5f14-86af-d1bf40ff6597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390321} {:answer/id #uuid "b95aeca7-2d75-536f-aad3-5ee534671a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bdc3170-5800-5005-80a5-41f4085c10cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390320} :answer-field/id #uuid "24525e5d-caa3-598a-bf9f-2cdde3684fdd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390318} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462e60fc-9628-5d57-98d0-760a3dc059df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390324} {:answer/id #uuid "62656bae-846a-540f-8c75-d6b19a51bf10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390325} {:answer/id #uuid "f3515f75-a357-5e54-b543-0943fd7d91a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:answer/id #uuid "8d4a4619-ff7d-58d9-b8fe-692fc7ccfd35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "462e60fc-9628-5d57-98d0-760a3dc059df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3k}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390324} :answer-field/id #uuid "31e88ca3-689c-5d6d-8d33-142250790a2b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390323} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "271998d6-6596-5766-a86c-eda65deb7648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390329} {:answer/id #uuid "22576850-98dc-5305-bc81-75d85a6f147c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{8}^{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390330} {:answer/id #uuid "74412403-68c5-5cab-a1b3-0e92c61a3892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3(1)-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74412403-68c5-5cab-a1b3-0e92c61a3892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{3(1)-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390331} :answer-field/id #uuid "71eb612b-ac6f-5dda-8e8f-e2b05b0b7c5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b22b72c6-05a2-5b9f-a118-f9a953c10650" :question/math-academy-id "q-183956" :question/problem "Consider the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=8{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{3n-3}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{3n-3}$ for all $n\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{3n-3}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1):\\,{a}_{1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{3(1)-3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=3\\cdot {2}^{3k-3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =3\\cdot {2}^{3(k+1)-3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{3k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}=3\\cdot {2}^{3n-3}$ for all $n\\in N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "607f5fc0-710c-50ec-853a-f1cbe588f0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390334} {:answer/id #uuid "f8387a2d-e7fd-51de-b176-ea56d9e540b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2eec72a5444533c1bb4f86836cf8479b76ae38d69c35f5f5eef5fd313a2365f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390335} {:answer/id #uuid "36db9686-690f-54ec-bfac-28a409caed44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/01d87db018bcbbf013d7f33519418849fe70e0d772870335482a7b07cfc89fd9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390336} {:answer/id #uuid "c165510a-291f-5076-b75f-5f046b3f04a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/70f7c02b3bca2b816858eaef766b5bbf3ced8d8abf101ef38ecda19b73781ee3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390337} {:answer/id #uuid "9f87f4ca-c61a-584a-8253-8ab30a3d9929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aa3411e662566a609de5163269dbc1e7c0ad92f415b52936c5ef568d7058d2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607f5fc0-710c-50ec-853a-f1cbe588f0cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390334} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a31ac2-1521-5379-9c4e-3e1335f990ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659e44f3-afb3-574d-a533-7467d2366762" :question/math-academy-id "q-184489" :question/problem "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S={-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ in the Cartesian plane?" :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\nIn this case, computing ${-1,2}\\times {-3,1}$ gives the following set:\n\n$$\nS={(-1,-3),(-1,1),(2,-3),(2,1)}.\n$$\n\nOur visualization of this set is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/09/09b74d8a7baf8c22cab4ad3841e53c7286cd72dd7523916544ea370595b199a9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc5f1e81-abf5-50d0-a56a-6bbee4a28f4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86cd6fb9fd52708dd41257a7df95db869ce6638e0dde902e2f7847d09021b530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390341} {:answer/id #uuid "542c23bb-867d-5c38-8c44-6f9ba3f17e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a713f172fca69aa423ae4664f2a6e31eb4315b666f17b9ea4a7c1ffbc8322be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390342} {:answer/id #uuid "8d51a496-cf22-5d8a-bc7b-ea2b23da875a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/748c2e450098f81b24d22a34ce375b1ea365c4879a33bfb2a539065e9e800e15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390343} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb92f3f-a231-5381-be9f-dc081efff8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390344} {:answer/id #uuid "6bea186d-febc-581c-a875-42e213a2d2a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d714297c6acc363f55a775fb3f6007145dec3aa415476d5c758f8c2def679ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb92f3f-a231-5381-be9f-dc081efff8bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390344} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc74910-d822-50ea-be12-c27bbc4353b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05268f20-60a4-56fb-b13c-c24e63cbe9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-184505" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the union of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *at least one* set:\n\n$$\n{⋃}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for at least one}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}={0.5}\\times [0,0.125]={(0.5,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,0.125]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(0.5,0)$ to the point $(0.5,0.125)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/3a/3a1bb7a7c54718e39df80092243a119ec8b5a6e77ec4f9d58ffb074e50191d04.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{3}]={(i,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,{i}^{3}]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{3}).$ \n\n![](images/47/472b6456019a5c89e585f80aa4a0dfd44f3b87c0624c10ea7b52f32e958a8c4e.png)\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following figure that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/20/204c9a31031534ac70a599322c3f77a077440300fde2364476643c88406e34cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933cab4-6c6f-5e1e-b9e9-375a48b8bbd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390348} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7f842-ec45-57a8-93f4-8edce627ac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/3357b2cf67b40afa54b081c67855f28a74d71209459382c0e421b6e2da7d114f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390349} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f47ff-1aa3-50e2-840e-6408b7c4640f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e802fe0b5558c1a076f65da60db5a24a58f667.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390350} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390351} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4a02-ebd9-56ca-9cfc-e2db41cc39a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d3/d3d7ea5224600381e2dd4f62618ce8a60804f89bb6949b82989017aa2556f87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff039e87-4af3-51fe-96a4-b9dddf2e393f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09d1de29-e974-591a-b590-9887d91abf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18523" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4dacecba4626f7c202777cad0a723295a2954f4b0c65fde83a81c6c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83423c03-d314-5f78-9acf-58cbb6f7774b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390354} {:answer/id #uuid "1a526c30-a852-56c6-b14c-c2025f540361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390355} {:answer/id #uuid "ab385b24-8ef8-5f1a-bd08-498b2099ce33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390356} {:answer/id #uuid "585a8c8c-14a7-54a3-b24f-e4f0fbf0ee6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390357} {:answer/id #uuid "353305b2-1bf3-530e-9fe4-e18cf98ffcba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{74}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{74}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f99cbb1-2bbb-59be-947b-044557644091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390354} :answer-field/id #uuid "13eb807a-8732-502e-90a4-aea8f6fe280d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46dce84e-4f15-50e3-8511-5dd62cf1728a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18975" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-2,8,3⟩.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\,| & =\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $a$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{77}}\\cdot ⟨-2,8,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{77}},\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{77}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d1dc808-1ee6-5037-b53e-9e1e4686a11c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Arcsecant Integral by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390361} {:answer/id #uuid "a0809cc0-8245-5717-8ded-90aaf0006a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x+6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390362} {:answer/id #uuid "de905a14-40b4-54a2-9966-938904217d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-4)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390363} {:answer/id #uuid "e79926dc-55a2-51bb-946e-9f9ee21d2e2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390364} {:answer/id #uuid "703b11b7-a06a-596e-9766-2f893cbd71bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x+2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd564d9f-63fb-590f-bd58-4b804a9ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390361} :answer-field/id #uuid "4529ba01-864b-583e-ae2f-75a351bb79bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db8ef128-2717-5091-8870-85aa35ff95e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-19532" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+35 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+({6}^{2}-{6}^{2})+35 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-12x+36)-36+35 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x=10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}\\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $x-6.$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=x-6.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|x-6|\\sqrt{(x-6)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{arcsec}(x-6)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "733eab11-2a26-5402-8df5-bba50c9beaf9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Cross Product Using Determinants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a2ecca-4420-5a73-85e5-d8fd94cb2fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390368} {:answer/id #uuid "37f1f0b7-f7d0-59d9-b906-b1e9b3bc1438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390369} {:answer/id #uuid "1b801238-dd43-58b7-a037-5fb7adad9f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-3,-15,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390370} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a830dc-88d1-5eec-a445-b8adc0a5469e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨15,5,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390371} {:answer/id #uuid "3abd8bb4-bf5c-5575-bf37-b9815f3df034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,5,12⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37f1f0b7-f7d0-59d9-b906-b1e9b3bc1438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-15,3,5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba37642-671c-52eb-9ccf-cac56d58f19b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765f0999-da93-5e44-b207-672576084830" :question/math-academy-id "q-19779" :question/problem "Let $a=⟨1,0,3⟩$ and $b=⟨2,5,3⟩.$ Find $a\\times b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 5)i-(1\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)j+(1\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =(0-15)i-(3-6)j+(5-0)k \\\\  & =⟨-15,3,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf440d3-3fd9-55cc-926d-c8fb116cd60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390375} {:answer/id #uuid "07658a20-9348-593b-a22c-bfd4aa0d76d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390376} {:answer/id #uuid "43daa5ce-d019-5af0-b38c-6fa388015557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390377} {:answer/id #uuid "ec19f197-25d0-50fa-b873-ca959251ea66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390378} {:answer/id #uuid "4c6061c2-cc6f-52e0-b686-f2b9c79ec854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43daa5ce-d019-5af0-b38c-6fa388015557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390377} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe07bb0-ff2d-54fa-ba57-0a37b56d9acc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a05ecba-dbf9-513d-af2d-03ee8f54c56e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20508" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8326c365-38e8-509f-ba26-6de3d26a5411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390382} {:answer/id #uuid "f0893938-c4dc-5b69-ac7a-20d7546db3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390383} {:answer/id #uuid "98de7d9c-ac96-50b3-a7cd-2e1f0a084bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390384} {:answer/id #uuid "5d46c30a-315a-5f3c-9e1c-febc548b0a21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390385} {:answer/id #uuid "c961a688-8a1a-5a32-8996-3f5dbb760b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0893938-c4dc-5b69-ac7a-20d7546db3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390383} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2efbab-ca3d-5dfb-bdbf-f29fc5964e47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b0ab2ea-5bc8-5361-b6e4-ca31cdf1350a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20539" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 9 & -13 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 17 & -26\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7294399b-b139-5ee6-824c-5e28e4fc49ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Reduced Row Echelon Form of a 3x3 System That Is Already in Row Echelon Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a91e48e3-62ec-5edb-bcf7-03d4a5806e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390389} {:answer/id #uuid "5461c0a1-245b-5870-8efa-189453e52726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390390} {:answer/id #uuid "0327ce18-3178-58df-99a3-60dc77f9fed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390391} {:answer/id #uuid "fabd327a-4307-5856-a87b-ef1b6f25459d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390392} {:answer/id #uuid "91ef62e9-3380-521c-8cbb-38d749ba6534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a91e48e3-62ec-5edb-bcf7-03d4a5806e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "M∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390389} :answer-field/id #uuid "13da932e-9b2a-5e64-abc3-8e5afbbc90ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f45c2e0-9678-5cdd-b7f1-5fd5cc96fa80" :question/math-academy-id "q-20590" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=0 \\\\  & y+2z=0 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form.\n\nTransforming $M$ to reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97cd9132-458b-5234-a3d2-22b633acb74c" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing an Augmented Matrix for a Singular 2x2 System to REF and Solving the Resulting System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9973610-47f9-5f62-a4ae-54d727ff19bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390396} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ddc9c8-cf3c-5347-a18e-5fc3f7cd82d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390397} {:answer/id #uuid "4142878a-1989-59e7-bb13-e08f2f47a895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390398} {:answer/id #uuid "15cec20b-2ee9-50b2-83e8-5d388cb51a75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390399} {:answer/id #uuid "2cc15e06-5b06-530d-86f3-88d4d6831e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9973610-47f9-5f62-a4ae-54d727ff19bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c5b0769-2362-5bb9-8ad0-cec516d68985" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0787607-5bca-5e97-b7f4-40db59c2425f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20963" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 16 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+2y=-1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-1-2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07f2ae4c-ff07-5b99-a7b0-90f460081eef" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Singular 2x2 System of Equations Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f60b20c1-50d7-5a28-8fb9-56799036743d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390403} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e39a9a-794e-5b5a-a3a6-c715ed28a2e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390404} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b88878-5d62-5aaf-940c-13f0db2b189c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390405} {:answer/id #uuid "f7da7299-b472-539e-9ead-96dba809c129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390406} {:answer/id #uuid "f107d4e1-af12-5629-9320-338119b98bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2b88878-5d62-5aaf-940c-13f0db2b189c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390405} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4a4ea00-9043-547d-b5fe-8dbfa8942263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1766ca49-3fde-5817-b4dd-1d97ee2424fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20965" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x+15y=10 \\\\ 2x+6y=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 15 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+3y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f742e7-89c6-543b-b43a-f41c27f35abf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Singular 3x3 System of Equations with One Pivot"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bda2c15e-c6cc-5b87-a877-08652a72a685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + y\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + z\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102777} {:answer/id #uuid "fc319139-6bb3-512b-9f22-587be6b62023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + y\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102778} {:answer/id #uuid "338c6eef-8bda-5265-95d1-76697464485d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102779} {:answer/id #uuid "5272eb86-b02b-51be-bd03-596750c818bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + y\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + z\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102780} {:answer/id #uuid "7454b053-9fed-557e-9165-14af5fe42a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + y\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + z\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y, z ∈ (- ∞, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "44bcb81c-f8b9-5e94-9d72-e527b31ec38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102776}] :question/id #uuid "93d7b7ec-0149-5969-9f8f-fedef6cf0475" :question/math-academy-id "q-20966" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd55c037-00e6-5219-914e-9a5a9bd7534e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e667849ed8a214e7d17871b0f96f73f83c59eec2677bfbaeaab589e04cc89e61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390410} {:answer/id #uuid "f174cb91-e1ee-5d6c-90ef-6029cbaebf29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/52266ff97300b29c85ac14f5b60b308d9e8a0e2fe3a01e8e0431b643e6a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390411} {:answer/id #uuid "071cdea1-0dac-5f25-b6f4-63481ba1e33c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a027e19617c0c62fd8f5247c6d1bbac894f9522ec9e90aec5b7ed05b3996b37.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390412} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9aba44-7003-5d32-9bce-752796690c23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cac3c2b4fb6191aa9a523b445d8533774f438d24556ea79b8e67a4d6c3867526.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390413} {:answer/id #uuid "38ce622d-f9f2-50a8-99cd-404657278c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f7/f7033ca262624a9723186563638f5ff006d4174a82349be0812ac3de4c25af5e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f174cb91-e1ee-5d6c-90ef-6029cbaebf29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/52/52266ff97300b29c85ac14f5b60b308d9e8a0e2fe3a01e8e0431b643e6a28922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390411} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cfcca37-0c5f-5e1f-94b7-02dbf414d41e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7e170ff-3ef3-51ef-bb76-d32ad791f4a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-21058" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fee79ec0b214a4ba87400b4b8c9dab2c623878d067821c572b26ae241625c48a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 15 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(2,10)$, $(3,15)$, and  $(5,25).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](images/bf/bf5987fde11416780a6a599e3952db7319e1c0e859e2439b7df70c5659bc6839.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a0fa5cb-58b2-5991-aeb3-3c72630b91f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080748} {:answer/id #uuid "d40ce140-4fa5-5e19-a6d2-c2ed4122946e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{2}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080749} {:answer/id #uuid "ebdb6ae8-5736-5ecb-a2b0-06e71866c8c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{3π}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080750} {:answer/id #uuid "1f118b88-363d-549d-8d78-43f7f7a3fe35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ and $t = \\frac{3π}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080751} {:answer/id #uuid "416acf6e-914c-5f39-8409-93bf2a0d33b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t = \\frac{π}{4}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080752}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b486e99f-74f6-58c2-873a-74bcf30e0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080747}] :question/id #uuid "fbd878d8-e318-5109-beb9-89ecefca7f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-21081" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2π$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8941003-fece-5a27-9a24-50a0d6f852dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390417} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7b79bb-d162-5d42-938f-17dc6295e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7b79bb-d162-5d42-938f-17dc6295e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390418} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd21228-5859-522b-b900-855a3b6d470d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afafe478-2e17-5ff9-82c1-667432a46587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390420} {:answer/id #uuid "00a59daf-ef53-5a0d-8f67-229640ca3ce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390421} {:answer/id #uuid "6c826f8f-5a6b-553e-85ef-a2a25222748f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} {:answer/id #uuid "b5af3a45-5715-50e4-a115-829d59895c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c826f8f-5a6b-553e-85ef-a2a25222748f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390422} :answer-field/id #uuid "478e490a-5240-599f-96de-4e191cdfa2a5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fae4dc0d-5fcc-556e-856f-aa7c560289fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-214907" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cb311261eabb4b5c1b6c4114c92e798f0caf2e326dc90486429d230167f6978b.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.The shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-2,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *right* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx>-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecbf45e4-67a6-58cd-9655-4a339de64db5" :knowledge-point/title "Inequalities Involving Horizontal and Vertical Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "658db8cb-ba47-576d-98bf-392b1c972351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390426} {:answer/id #uuid "9a199d3b-122e-5520-ab63-cad8960ce2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390427} {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390428} {:answer/id #uuid "823ad81d-b696-522f-ab58-29ea625c84a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68d8126-7623-5eaf-bd8f-5ac5d9474dda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390428} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a5871fe-f6ee-5364-9ea7-6831196e61f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "603761b4-58e0-582d-bb8b-85f71e2feec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390431} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4374b4-87ee-5f43-8d23-b5167780b2a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390432} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba292289-f6b9-5d5c-b56b-a6376634f5af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84d30d8f-6033-5842-95cc-601ebb4c6d51" :question/math-academy-id "q-215437" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb24be727a3bb001e25b5f2c758b1302332aa8b45a51cfd0d4884b2784479e58.png)\n\nExpress the shaded region above as an inequality.\nThe shaded region above is described by the inequality\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The border-line is a vertical line passing through the point $(-1,0).$ Therefore, the line has the equation $x=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is solid, we will use an \"or equal\" inequality. Since the shaded region is to the *left* of the line, the inequality refers to the $x$-values that are *less than or equal to* those on the line $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\nx\\le -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4598f8c6-29a4-5f54-b333-609d74d34044" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Formula For Negating a Quantified Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1fff4c0-1c35-5d96-ac98-1db607a1a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390435} {:answer/id #uuid "1a24afea-7619-549c-887e-2f51656a24f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∃" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1fff4c0-1c35-5d96-ac98-1db607a1a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∀" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390435} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4020bca-3a49-51fd-a6d8-bc2cf390a70a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c5edc3-dd10-5caf-9549-46e2e61777ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390438} {:answer/id #uuid "d67e3e45-d944-54df-829b-a8366b7a84dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬P(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c5edc3-dd10-5caf-9549-46e2e61777ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390438} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a5c5fa5-cf04-545b-9d1a-542a247bbf1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d856d4a-9fe5-5cdd-af82-e60a7dd824fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-216588" :question/problem "$¬(∃y\\in \\overset{―}{N},\\,¬P(y))\\,≡${{field-1}} $y\\in \\overset{―}{N}$, {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To negate the existential statement $∃y\\in \\overset{―}{N},\\,¬P(y),$ we flip the quantifier to universal and negate the predicate: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃y\\in \\overset{―}{N},\\,¬P(y)) & ≡∀y\\in \\overset{―}{N},\\,¬¬P(y) \\\\  & ≡∀y\\in \\overset{―}{N},\\,P(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Equation of a Line Given a Point and Direction Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72cf5b47-1d96-537e-965e-8add5cfd6d5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨8,0,-5⟩+t⟨1,-2,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390442} {:answer/id #uuid "a63d7512-9445-5a60-bc75-71f99f5daaab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨8,0,-5⟩+t⟨-2,1,8⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390443} {:answer/id #uuid "a919b853-1e14-54b0-96bd-94ffc0b7a338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨1,8,-2⟩+t⟨8,-5,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390444} {:answer/id #uuid "015fd8c4-fc95-5238-92fc-e670605ee584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+⟨8,0,-5⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390445} {:answer/id #uuid "cd92859a-d3fb-5aab-bd1d-d4196785ac43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd92859a-d3fb-5aab-bd1d-d4196785ac43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390446} :answer-field/id #uuid "08e34971-c8a1-582a-ad3f-efdfbdaeb1f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a298fdc-2b4e-5d13-9ff1-366f2f62e2b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-22285" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the straight line passing through $P(-2,1,8)$ and parallel to $v=⟨8,0,-5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nIn our case, the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$\n\nUsing the formula for the vector equation of a line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨-2,1,8⟩+t⟨8,0,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e11493-093d-5677-9cce-796074ba130e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061756} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d8e750-c89b-5ba7-b458-68ac026f484a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061757} {:answer/id #uuid "d85f6949-16e9-59c0-8f4b-cd4eba8557d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061758} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8ae605-3c4c-5bbe-bc7a-ba41af30bda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061759} {:answer/id #uuid "7aec39cd-06b6-51e7-8fac-330d7d637bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061760}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6adffb-ec69-5386-895c-90f005cfeb37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061755}] :question/id #uuid "542cb4a0-6380-5eaf-91e8-44f9300f10c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-22428" :question/problem "For the function $f(x) = x - 2\\ln x$, defined on the closed interval $\\frac{1}{2} \\le x \\le e$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The global maximum value is $\\frac{1}{2} + 2\\ln 2$\n2. The global minimum value is $e - 2$\n3. The global minimum value is $2 - 2\\ln 2$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d65819f-0528-527e-8d1a-20421e221ca5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Coterminal Angles in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d432b851-5f5b-5ae5-be26-4e0689222795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390449} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d89b72-0360-5da3-ade1-fefd132aa0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390450} {:answer/id #uuid "577f3889-2b45-5558-904e-e2cbac624263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390451} {:answer/id #uuid "0c6cd02a-2f0d-503e-a37c-d8406949c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390452} {:answer/id #uuid "f869e96c-c634-5b77-b46e-622984f3215c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d89b72-0360-5da3-ade1-fefd132aa0d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390450} :answer-field/id #uuid "323d55c4-1afd-5a97-b428-98053114cd7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72a396d7-e8cf-5458-9d0a-c10ca03427ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-22984" :question/problem "Which of the following angles are coterminal with $\\theta =2\\pi $\n\n- $4\\pi $\n- $5\\pi $\n- $-2\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "To find the coterminal angles of a given angle $\\theta $, we add and subtract integer multiples of $2\\pi :$\n\nLet's start by adding multiples of $2\\pi $ to $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\pi =4\\pi \\,✓$\n\n- $2\\pi +2\\cdot (2\\pi )=6\\pi $\n\nOf the given values, there is no angle greater than $6\\pi .$ So, we can stop there.\n\nNow, let's subtract multiples of $2\\pi $ from $2\\pi $:\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\pi =0$\n\n- $2\\pi -2\\cdot (2\\pi )=-2\\pi \\,✓$\n\nOf the given angles, only $4\\pi $ and $-2\\pi $ are coterminal with $2\\pi .$\n\nSo the correct answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b60859c-9313-57c4-ab2c-b6301ec7a585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b60859c-9313-57c4-ab2c-b6301ec7a585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390456} :answer-field/id #uuid "693df243-e3aa-5bc3-b0ad-1f9b556c185b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390455} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7de0996-a9fe-586d-be3e-7283cf5ede7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7de0996-a9fe-586d-be3e-7283cf5ede7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390458} :answer-field/id #uuid "caadb3ad-24f0-5d49-b782-706543f5f1d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0f5813f-1311-5202-9907-b6bbfa128b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-250543" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${x}^{2}-12x+40=0.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-12,$ and $c=40.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^{2}-4(1)(40)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{144-160}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=6+2\\text{i}$ and $x=6-2\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5556a7ad-095a-5e96-a10f-546b646f387a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5556a7ad-095a-5e96-a10f-546b646f387a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390461} :answer-field/id #uuid "16f4b867-03a3-5cf3-b36b-fe6dd6d8c862" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390460} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9bd8c4-9924-59c8-ac3c-879c2fb180ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9bd8c4-9924-59c8-ac3c-879c2fb180ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390463} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e4d363a-dae6-552a-afbf-9a57d3d5e887" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a8f88f4-fdb0-54c4-b806-363e999f0b6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-250552" :question/problem "Solve the equation $12x={x}^{2}+61.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+12x-61=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=-1,\\,b=12,$ and $c=-61.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-12\\pm \\sqrt{(12)^{2}-4(-1)(-61)}}{2(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12\\pm \\sqrt{144-244}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12\\pm 10\\,\\text{i}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =6\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=6+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=6-5\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07db4b9-ac89-59f7-9d33-9dfb4e0ed947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390465} {:answer/id #uuid "f0baa69d-2278-57c4-b35a-4be6001576c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390466} {:answer/id #uuid "4d81d875-d6cf-5cd5-bc0d-5a4f7e51ad2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390467} {:answer/id #uuid "c7187480-ce34-508c-b3ec-9aca89a490db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390468} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd0b52a-8a06-56ea-b802-eaecc89cba1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7187480-ce34-508c-b3ec-9aca89a490db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d866f6d2-3a06-5422-82c3-a9cfd7fd2522" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8fbea78d-a11e-5e1b-9dbc-9e0727e11887" :question/math-academy-id "q-252922" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $B=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $\\operatorname{ln}⁡B.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and the given matrix $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡B=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡B & =P\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39069774-9c46-5d4f-862a-c1c25ae485ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390471} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2a41fb-db96-57b0-bd5f-3cbded77b7aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390472} {:answer/id #uuid "43c23dca-e7c5-57f0-894a-bde7af40459e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43c23dca-e7c5-57f0-894a-bde7af40459e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390473} :answer-field/id #uuid "2638ede2-dd06-5d01-af9b-929e90885a93" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb49ea74-c1da-568a-8c56-637343257682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390475} {:answer/id #uuid "def99a31-0354-5db3-9ddd-9228fc75443a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def99a31-0354-5db3-9ddd-9228fc75443a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390476} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b22d6b0-03bd-5d22-b87a-28363f79e026" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390474} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feba7b67-a277-5854-a9bc-2e6d354c5177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390478} {:answer/id #uuid "8d32587b-4e2e-526e-a5f9-f0b6077e6e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feba7b67-a277-5854-a9bc-2e6d354c5177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390478} :answer-field/id #uuid "e330f5e5-8bb0-5450-a091-0346ebe1feef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "958c8b51-02a6-562a-b91d-0cb22577bebf" :question/math-academy-id "q-253360" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51142747-7995-5b64-8484-00f92ec9c045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390482} {:answer/id #uuid "af673e15-2f46-587c-b1de-bba4cb3584a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390483} {:answer/id #uuid "40e1e4fb-2688-53cf-b12e-229cd75dad92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af673e15-2f46-587c-b1de-bba4cb3584a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390483} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc583fcf-a96e-5934-98c7-d7549059f10e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390481} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069c5a8d-3b99-569f-bd15-a89cb69ee2b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390486} {:answer/id #uuid "9e414f21-06a9-5bfc-bf80-da36fe5769e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e414f21-06a9-5bfc-bf80-da36fe5769e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390487} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0b6f51-f80a-5af1-920b-c91fe049b4d2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73fe995d-577f-5ed8-90c6-0af457a3273f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390489} {:answer/id #uuid "b42d7491-72fd-5c77-83ed-32b644bfc11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73fe995d-577f-5ed8-90c6-0af457a3273f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390489} :answer-field/id #uuid "c71eaeb6-b41a-5238-b635-a15bfe1a6145" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1ec1408-7504-5b11-90fb-9d3bb0345917" :question/math-academy-id "q-253362" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb02d1ee-e874-56ee-9c31-9b099e11c6ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390493} {:answer/id #uuid "3867f9a1-a810-56f4-a7b7-b06c990c71a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390494} {:answer/id #uuid "6df1956e-364a-5a9a-881a-6421ee452c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390495} {:answer/id #uuid "526ac3c4-7ca1-57b6-9892-c6c51a1de885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390496} {:answer/id #uuid "af136552-a664-56da-af57-825d45a55ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df1956e-364a-5a9a-881a-6421ee452c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390495} :answer-field/id #uuid "db812932-512e-5037-a8e9-0e6ef6900bbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bf7af58-a7c5-5140-8c49-6a540dd66971" :question/math-academy-id "q-253440" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find ${2}^{A}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and the given matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{A}={2}^{(PD{P}^{-1})}=P\\cdot ({2}^{D})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n{2}^{D}=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{A} & =P\\cdot ({2}^{D})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11616b01-2dc9-5583-b247-44011865d37e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\pi +6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390500} {:answer/id #uuid "347181c6-f735-53cd-9e7a-c9a179a7fb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390501} {:answer/id #uuid "97728fa4-9b8c-5b3f-bfe8-e7cd26108fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390502} {:answer/id #uuid "8351c2fe-bb03-587a-86e5-dc4f192359bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\pi +3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 2\\pi -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390503} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc015e4-fa81-5911-b453-9b01ae821ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi -3 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi +3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "347181c6-f735-53cd-9e7a-c9a179a7fb52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390501} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fe431d2-c2a4-554b-8c9d-25c08aebb39d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "462adc62-6b27-5233-9c9d-d820967cef9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-253677" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nG=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+2 & \\,\\,-2 \\\\ 2 & \\,\\,\\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\,1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $G=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find $12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and the given matrix $G,$ we have\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G=12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(PJ{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $\\sqrt{3}$ on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+1}$ So, we get\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\sqrt{3})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡G & =P\\cdot (12\\operatorname{arctan}⁡J)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4\\pi  & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\pi \\end{aligned}]\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi +3 & 4\\pi -3 \\\\ 4\\pi  & -4\\pi \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\pi +6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\pi -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "706303c1-0e4c-55f7-8b62-5094938f85bc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Value of a Function of a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a046da-5836-5310-b035-b3ab87808bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9 \\\\ -9 & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390507} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4b63d5-d2f6-5ba0-9f3d-4114e4e39210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390508} {:answer/id #uuid "68fa6317-9f05-5a88-b253-5911ad78919f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390509} {:answer/id #uuid "bc33a49f-e4db-52ac-8dc1-e9a2a9bfa5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390510} {:answer/id #uuid "c46be42c-dda5-53c8-9a44-89e57ebf0b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390511}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c46be42c-dda5-53c8-9a44-89e57ebf0b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390511} :answer-field/id #uuid "39a6382b-3f11-58a5-8769-fb40b3f511f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "01aa6efd-1aa0-5169-9c59-06c6dc6237ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-253682" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $K=PJ{P}^{-1},$ find ${3}^{K/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)={3}^{x/2}$ and the given matrix $K,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{K/2}={3}^{\\frac{1}{2}PJ{P}^{-1}}=P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $J$ is a Jordan block with $4$s on the main diagonal, we have\n\n$$\nf(J)=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $f$ is ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2}.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n{3}^{J/2}=[\\begin{aligned}f(4) & {f}^{′}(4) \\\\ 0 & f(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{K/2} & =P\\cdot ({3}^{J/2})\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & \\frac{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{2} \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)-9 \\\\ -18 & 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & 9+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7" :knowledge-point/title "Fully Classifying Recurrence Relations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a70412e4-2e2f-509d-b258-df889378ed00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390514} {:answer/id #uuid "2705c2a3-8a66-5c84-a42d-4a3bc20f8cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390515} {:answer/id #uuid "978fdd34-d68a-507b-97a8-caec50bc0c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390516}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2705c2a3-8a66-5c84-a42d-4a3bc20f8cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390515} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f0e00d-e20e-5982-94a6-5289f5aa4b11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a65574e-8a62-5a6a-8bc4-abba62fbf917" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390518} {:answer/id #uuid "9eded351-6c30-5dd3-bee3-4404ef3f23a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eded351-6c30-5dd3-bee3-4404ef3f23a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "non-linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390519} :answer-field/id #uuid "b394bb76-f844-516f-b53c-d9c74f9b00ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "29f7e0a9-64b5-586d-8f67-de40eb741d00" :question/math-academy-id "q-265151" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and\nis\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The term ${a}_{n-1}$ is not in the first power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is\n$\\text{non-linear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db17739-2def-5e37-af22-164593bd5829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/61cd6304483770e91658f37cd8ac3dbf57c58f25c9a631ab45a56cd706d381ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390522} {:answer/id #uuid "9904a233-df69-591c-90af-01e22fb617bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/31f0d95293939e141e0ebff2aaad6223a9a82188e892441906ae09c530919b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390523} {:answer/id #uuid "acd865cc-8ca0-5df0-b0c5-c2cd29210f44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/dfb46737087c1196ce079607220107a88857d6ca139d0d5c53b181795fe1344a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390524} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc2a988-d900-5cd3-a9a0-9a6ecf303b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eef682605cf7486a9d8fa23cd508705e70443622164eeb2c253ca342bbce92cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390525} {:answer/id #uuid "fffb642d-e2f2-5134-92b3-6bde0b2a9298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce2bc405efee4fc0fc0f8cd53a3f230fe407f1232c98237ced4ce64743a8499.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fffb642d-e2f2-5134-92b3-6bde0b2a9298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce2bc405efee4fc0fc0f8cd53a3f230fe407f1232c98237ced4ce64743a8499.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390526} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0ebeea-e82f-58d4-a5b0-0f856e91026c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390521}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63caaf01-44b4-5dc6-a569-9e3a3a5c8859" :question/math-academy-id "q-26811" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={7}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/cd/cd8226319b26b3fef6db6e98efc187b1c474c91bc0252e424151791fda373b95.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/85/85cc1403ab74e2df97669a7c3363d1abddf6d093c358e888a69b655f1ca7450a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4093346-6cc0-5f63-9423-7b76da2a672f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1d80c585063f3cd30a6ec1f877e0f2add2bf109bc54506e4b6c9e44a7b5019d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390529} {:answer/id #uuid "fd28d0fd-5ee9-586e-ade7-f559ef7573ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bbdfe3e538e840385c082f16ca2cdfd07a7731c561d05cab6e7a25f487cdf463.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390530} {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390531} {:answer/id #uuid "35b1a419-9084-5fab-a034-b69305bdd882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6674add26b7a15f5b09c53a832d649979484f0ffd41b440069dd5230cd9a5187.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390532} {:answer/id #uuid "724df295-2359-5b8b-ab62-27d9d0e10f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a30938f3d3af2fd70a0ee9498d7d99973d5386f062f22aaa8a028e9e18b3fb57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390531} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb7b8b6-ddf5-57db-b6c9-dfd64245bb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5100b042-b82c-5834-8756-fafba09daf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26815" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/29/29d270676a48f4cf40625ef3ec50b0323c9c99389b2a7d080881727eda28de56.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75,$ we take the graph of $y={6}^{x}$ and shift it $0.75$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0.25),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.75.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b141be99e5c01c70d6f6f04f4adf06741f1dc0fcef80c73b52e3dba1ae44a87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858c261e-14be-5341-b40a-d550c565ee07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "858c261e-14be-5341-b40a-d550c565ee07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390536} :answer-field/id #uuid "ace71b7c-8f1f-5f7b-b5d8-3a10944ba75b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "612bf785-43c9-5609-bd69-355f15a47984" :question/math-academy-id "q-270439" :question/problem "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=49{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+7$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $7,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 7.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $49,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$, $\\pm 7$ and $\\pm 49.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{7}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{7}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to their lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{49}, \\\\ \\pm 7, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{49},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{7},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 7.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $8$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 4=8.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145dde8-60d2-54b7-ad8d-2892ff5d3963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4161037f51b8c4d95037c3ff28d7e6afecb354d4e0fabc22e5dd819bb65b6cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390539} {:answer/id #uuid "6680ac81-034b-5243-a46f-e14b15ca391f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4511ffac1899bdeb512b0b50b50b6a121477f486ef7e2efc83697fd9dddb8d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390540} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b451c-b477-571c-b8f0-b6b471ddf99b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/953bb642696a6b9c934627f978d150d27adbc7f73779a3d017c2ba714ff0835e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390541} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd2d67a-323e-5832-a842-a36b0a6fce73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answ